</section>
<section id="S5" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">5 </span>Conclusion</h2>

<div id="S5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We conducted a study on log-perplexity as a measure of gibberishness in audio, adapting various <a href="#id25"><abbr href="#id25" title="Are These Even Words? Quantifying the Gibberishnessof Generative Speech Models" class="ltx_glossaryref ltx_missing">SpeechLM</abbr></a> for this task. We found SpeechLMScore to have better performance when using discrete tokens from the 6th layer rather than the 3rd, although it still fails to make a clear distinction between meaningful and gibberish speech. In a conventional noisy mixture setting, SpeechGPT was the best in correlation with the intrusive <a href="#id3"><abbr href="#id3" title="Are These Even Words? Quantifying the Gibberishnessof Generative Speech Models" class="ltx_glossaryref ltx_missing">PER</abbr></a>. Overall, the most balanced method was TWIST, with consistent performance across all experiments. Finally, we published a test set of high-quality synthesized gibberish samples for further development of methods for assessment of gibberishness in speech.</p>
</div>
<div class="ltx_pagination ltx_role_newpage"></div>
</section>
